Package: cli-image-paste
Version: @VERSION@
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Architecture: all
Maintainer: Andrea Bonacci <andreabonacci95@protonmail.com>
Homepage: https://github.com/AndreaBonn/cli-image-paste
Depends: bash (>= 4.4), xclip | wl-clipboard
Recommends: imagemagick, libnotify-bin
Suggests: xdotool, wtype, librsvg2-bin, grim, slurp, ydotool
Description: paste clipboard images into the active terminal as a file path
 CLI coding assistants accept image files as input but have no way to read
 an image from the system clipboard. This tool bridges the gap: copy an
 image, press a shortcut, and the path to a saved file is typed into the
 terminal that had focus.
 .
 Reading the clipboard and delivering the path are separate: xclip on X11,
 wl-clipboard on Wayland, and delivery through xdotool, wtype or the
 clipboard itself depending on what the compositor allows. On GNOME Wayland,
 where no tool can synthesise keystrokes, the path is placed in the clipboard
 and a notification says so.
 .
 The package installs the executable only. Registering a keyboard shortcut is
 left to the user, since the package cannot know which desktop is running at
 install time: run paste-image --print-shortcut for the config line, or use
 the installer from the source tree.
